{"kind":"report","text":"LL-1 measured the native installed lease fixture at published 02d93554. The test packet admits 64 cards and gives every measured member the real installed action grant. Each distinct member joined through the native invitation path and received a session. The effective logical cap was 33,554,432 bytes. New generations started at 5,837 bytes. The runs measured three renewal rounds at each cadence with 22-character fresh keys. Exact retries returned the original ticks and added zero bytes.\n\n| Members | After enter, bytes | Samples per interval | Bytes per renewal | Projected hours at 15 s | Projected hours at 25 s |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 273,672 | 3 | 356 | 389.52 | 649.20 |\n| 5 | 1,344,438 | 15 | 356–358 | 74.98–75.40 | 124.96–125.66 |\n| 50 | 13,391,179 | 150 | 358–360 | 4.67–4.69 | 7.78–7.82 |\n\nThe hours use floor((cap − after-enter bytes) / sampled renewal bytes) × interval / (3600 × members), evaluated at the sampled endpoints. They assume constant charges, no churn and no competing ceiling. The real-cap runs stop after three rounds. Values characterize this installed fixture, including the two requested cadences.\n\nI propose an explicit declared live lease-renewal act. Its declaration names the fixed required member slot and the time deadline. The validator accepts only a transition of one active item with a signer-equals-owner guard, a required expected revision, and a sole bounded time extension on the deadline. The matching timed rule finishes the item. The act carries no other effects, sends, external marks or attention. A viewport-changing update uses the ordinary live act path. Give a renewal a signed key derived from the full scope, current generation, item and expected revision in a separate renewal identity domain.\n\nThe live turn checks generation and current member authority. The fold atomically replaces the item’s one latest renewal record, containing the signed digest, actor, expected revision and applied tick, while updating the deadline. With the same valid generation and authority, repeating the exact latest signed intent returns its original applied tick and changes neither the item nor meter. Different bytes under that latest identity use the existing live refusal, bad-intent with name idempotency-mismatch. A stale requested revision that the record cannot prove latest answers the existing revision-moved refusal and changes neither the item nor meter. It can deny a stale attempt regardless of whether it previously applied. Rotation and revocation use current member authority. Keep the one record for each item through the generation, including after expiry, and bound it in the existing logical meter. The ordinary live-act accepted index and its exact-retry guarantee keep their existing semantics.\n\nExpected derive owners are validate/handlers.ts, validate/format-1.ts, validate/live-envelope.ts and validate/live-meter.ts for the admitted form and its bound; judge.ts for authorization and identity; state.ts, live-stage.ts and fold.ts for the one retained record and atomic replacement. packages/scope/src/live.ts owns admission, authority, the latest reply and the staged application. The added declaration belongs in packages/contract/src/definition.ts. The live responses reuse the existing LiveAnswer reasons. The checker reviews the new form and changed live judgment under docs/kernel.md section 7. The later note amendment will follow the planner’s ruling.\n\nA separate lowered-cap native control set the test limit to 274,384 bytes after entering at 273,672. Two 356-byte renewals fit through tick 4. The third fresh intent returned scope-full with name liveBytes at tick 4 and the same byte count. Repeating the second signed intent returned applied tick 4 at the same meter value. The configured 32 MiB value stayed the basis for the table above.\n\nEvidence is /Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-21-ll1-renewal-measurement. The pilot log unde